# 6. International maritime law

This is one of the most important areas because a blockade is not simply a matter of declaring that a coastline is closed.

| Term | Definition |
| --- | --- |
| Law of Naval Warfare | International legal rules governing naval conflict. |
| Law of Armed Conflict (LOAC) | Rules governing the conduct of parties during armed conflict. |
| International Humanitarian Law (IHL) | Law protecting people affected by armed conflict. |
| UNCLOS | United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea. |
| San Remo Manual | Influential nonbinding expert manual addressing international law applicable to armed conflicts at sea. |
| Declaration of Paris (1856) | International agreement addressing maritime warfare and privateering. |
| Neutrality | Legal status of a state that does not participate in an armed conflict. |
| Neutral Shipping | Commercial shipping associated with neutral states. |
| Contraband | Goods that may be subject to restrictions under applicable wartime legal rules. |
| Visit and Search | A historically recognized procedure for examining vessels under specified legal circumstances. |
| Prize Law | Legal framework concerning capture of enemy vessels and cargo. |
| Prize Court | Court that determines the legality of maritime captures. |
| Humanitarian Exemption | Provision allowing specified humanitarian activities despite restrictions. |
| Proportionality | Requirement that certain military actions not cause excessive incidental civilian harm relative to anticipated military advantage. |
| Distinction | Requirement to distinguish between civilians and combatants, and civilian objects and military objectives. |
| Neutral Rights | Rights and protections applicable to neutral states under international law. |

Important distinction: A naval blockade during an armed conflict, economic sanctions, and peacetime restrictions on maritime trade are different legal instruments. They should not be treated as interchangeable.

